course: space
lesson: 16
title: Meteors, meteorites and shooting stars
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A shooting star is not a star at all: it is a grain of rock or dust, often smaller than a grain of sand, rushing into the Earth's air so fast that it glows and burns up.
explain: The same piece has three names: a meteoroid while it is in space, a meteor while it glows in the air, and a meteorite if any of it lands on the ground.
explain: Rub your hands together hard and they get warm: a meteor hits the air at tens of kilometres every second, far faster than a bullet, and the air in front of it is squashed so hard it glows.
explain: Meteor showers come on about the same dates each year when the Earth passes through a dust trail, and all their meteors seem to fly out of one point, the radiant.
explain: Big impacts are rare but real: one blasted out Meteor Crater in Arizona, and a far bigger one about 66 million years ago helped end the non-bird dinosaurs.
fact: A shooting star is not a star at all but a tiny grain of rock or dust, often smaller than a grain of sand, burning up as it rushes into the Earth's air.
fact: A piece of space rock is a meteoroid while in space, a meteor while it glows in the air, and a meteorite if any of it lands on the ground.
fact: Meteors enter the air at between about 11 and 72 kilometres per second, many times faster than a bullet.
fact: Most meteors glow and burn out between about 70 and 120 kilometres up, far above aeroplanes and clouds.
fact: Meteor showers happen on about the same dates each year when the Earth passes through a trail of dust left by a comet, such as the Perseids around 12 August from comet Swift-Tuttle.
fact: The Geminids, peaking around 13 to 14 December, are one of the richest showers of the year and come from an asteroid called 3200 Phaethon rather than a comet.
fact: The meteors of a shower seem to fly out from one point in the sky, called the radiant, though they can streak across any part of the sky, and the shower is named after the constellation, the named star pattern, where that point lies.
fact: A fireball is a meteor brighter than the planet Venus, made by a larger lump, and it can sometimes break up with a boom heard on the ground.
fact: In February 2021 a meteorite fell on a driveway in Winchcombe, Gloucestershire, and was picked up within hours, one of the freshest carbon-rich meteorites ever found.
fact: Most meteorites are chips of asteroids from the belt between Mars and Jupiter.
fact: A few hundred known meteorites were blasted off the Moon or Mars.
fact: Meteor Crater in Arizona, about 1.2 kilometres wide, was blasted out roughly 50,000 years ago.
fact: An impact about 66 million years ago helped end the age of the non-bird dinosaurs.
exercise: What is a shooting star really? | A tiny grain of rock or dust burning up in the air | A star falling out of the sky | A small star exploding far away | A spark from an aeroplane engine
exercise: What is a piece of space rock called while it is still in space? | A meteoroid | A meteor | A meteorite | A comet tail
exercise: What is it called while it glows in the air? | A meteor | A meteoroid | A meteorite | A planet
exercise: What is it called if part of it lands on the ground? | A meteorite | A meteor | A meteoroid | A shooting star
exercise: How fast do meteors enter the air? | About 11 to 72 kilometres per second | About as fast as a car on a motorway | About as fast as a jumbo jet | Slower than a falling leaf
exercise: How high up do most meteors burn out? | About 70 to 120 kilometres up | Just above the treetops, low down | At about the height of the clouds | Halfway to the Moon, far out in space
exercise: What are the Perseids? | A shower around 12 August from comet Swift-Tuttle | A shower in December from an asteroid | A group of moons of Jupiter | A comet that comes back around once every 76 years
exercise: Where do the Geminid meteors come from? | An asteroid called 3200 Phaethon | A comet called Halley, every 76 years | The red planet Mars and its two moons | Saturn's rings, as they slowly break up
exercise: When do the Geminids peak? | Around 13 to 14 December | Around 12 August each summer | Around 1 January, New Year's Day | Around midsummer, in late June
exercise: What is the radiant of a meteor shower? | The point in the sky the meteors seem to fly out from | The brightest meteor of the night | The crater a meteorite makes | The tail of the comet that left the dust
exercise: How does a meteor shower get its name? | From the constellation where its radiant lies | From the person who saw it first and wrote it down | From the month it happens in | From the colour of its meteors
exercise: What is a fireball? | A meteor brighter than the planet Venus | A meteor that sets fire to trees where it lands | A small star that has come very close to the Earth | A comet's glowing head, without its long tail
exercise: What happened in Winchcombe, Gloucestershire, in February 2021? | A carbon-rich meteorite fell on a driveway and was picked up within hours | A meteor shower was named after the town | A fireball set fire to a farm | A comet passed so close it was seen by day
exercise: Where do most meteorites come from? | Chips of asteroids between Mars and Jupiter | Pieces of the Sun | Pieces of shooting stars from other galaxies | Dust from Saturn's rings
exercise: Where did a few hundred known meteorites come from? | The Moon or Mars | Jupiter's clouds | The Sun's surface | The Earth's own volcanoes
exercise: How big is Meteor Crater in Arizona? | About 1.2 kilometres wide | About 12 metres wide | About 1,200 kilometres wide | About the size of a football pitch
exercise: What did an impact about 66 million years ago help to do? | End the age of the non-bird dinosaurs | Make the Moon out of the bits that flew off | Start the very first life on the young Earth | Make the rings around Saturn out of rock
test: My mate made a wish on a shooting star and said a star died for it. what's actually going on | A grain of dust burnt up high in the air | A real star fell all the way down to the Earth | A star exploded right next to us | A satellite crashed into the Moon
test: Why do meteors burn up so high, far above the aeroplanes? | They hit the air so fast that it glows round them high up | Aeroplanes shoot them down | They are lit by the Sun only up there | They bounce off the clouds
test: A lump of rock is glowing across the sky. Ten seconds later a piece lands in a field. Name the two stages. | First a meteor, then a meteorite | First a meteorite, then a meteor | First a comet, then a star | First a meteoroid, then a comet
test: In a film a shooting star is shown as a big rock crashing into a town every night. What is wrong? | Almost all meteors are tiny grains that burn up high | Nothing, that happens every night | Shooting stars only hit the sea | Shooting stars are made of ice that melts
test: If you face the wrong way on Perseid night, will you miss all the meteors? | No, they can cross any part of the sky though they seem to come from one point | Yes, they only appear right at the radiant | Yes, you must look straight at the Moon | No, because they all fall towards the ground near you
test: ugh, my brother says all meteor showers come from comets. right or not | Not all, the Geminids come from an asteroid | Yes, every single one comes from a comet's tail | No, they all come from Mars | No, they come from the Moon
test: You hear a boom and see a meteor brighter than Venus. What did you see? | A fireball, made by a larger lump | An ordinary tiny shooting star | A star exploding | A plane breaking the sound barrier
test: A meteorite turns out to be just like the rocks brought back by the Apollo astronauts. Where did it most likely come from? | The Moon | Jupiter | The Sun | Saturn's rings
test: Why was the Winchcombe meteorite so valuable to scientists? | It was found within hours, so it was very fresh | It was made of solid gold, right the way through it | It was the biggest meteorite that anyone has ever found | It came all the way from another galaxy far from ours
test: Which is true? | Big impacts are rare, but they have happened | Big impacts happen every week | Meteorites have never hit the Earth | Meteor Crater was made by a volcano, not by a meteorite
test: crap, the dinosaurs died from a meteorite? all of 'em? | An impact helped end the non-bird dinosaurs; birds are their living relatives | Yes, every dinosaur and every bird died at once | No, dinosaurs never met any meteorite | Yes, and it happened 50,000 years ago
exam: Identify the designation applied to a body of extraterrestrial rock which has reached the surface of the Earth. | A meteorite | A meteor | A meteoroid | A comet
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the nature of so-called shooting stars. | They are small particles of rock or dust incinerated in the atmosphere | They are stars descending towards the Earth | They are distant stars undergoing explosion | They are reflections of moonlight upon clouds
exam: State the term employed to denote the point in the sky from which the meteors of a shower appear to originate. | The radiant | The zenith | The horizon | The equinox
exam: Ascertain the parent body of the meteor shower designated the Geminids. | The asteroid 3200 Phaethon | The comet Swift-Tuttle | The comet Halley, which comes every 76 years | The planet Mars
